#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128943 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c1723a1-c676-517c-8dc5-f2b61ba017c6" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral by Swapping the Order of Integration"}] :question/id #uuid "9b2415da-21b8-5a59-9b3f-2f30b624c36f" :question/math-academy-id "e-7194" :question/problem "Change the order of integration and evaluate the double integral $\\displaystyle{\\int_0^{1} \\int_{\\sqrt{x}}^{1} 9\\sqrt{y^3+1}\\,\\textrm{d}y\\,\\textrm{d}x}$." :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1997/Source/Images/b061a691651d115b218622521988a608.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. This is a type I region, and its type I representation is\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{(x,y) \\::\\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\quad \\sqrt{x} \\leq y \\leq 1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x)$, is\n\n$$\nx=0\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x)$, is\n\n$$\ny = \\sqrt{x} \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=y^2\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as type II region. Its type II representation is\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{(x,y) \\::\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\quad 0 \\leq x \\leq y^2 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1997/Source/Images/a2cd4b5f56b6c6f034e5205b72232a90.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{1}∫_{\\sqrt{x}}^{1}9\\sqrt{y^{3} + 1}dydx &= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{y^{2}}9\\sqrt{y^{3} + 1}dxdy \\\\\n&= 9∫_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{y^{3} + 1}[∫_{0}^{y^{2}}dx]dy \\\\\n&= 9∫_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{y^{3} + 1}[x]_{0}^{y^{2}}dy \\\\\n&= 3∫_{0}^{1}3y^{2}(y^{3} + 1)^{1/2}dy \\\\\n&= 3[\\frac{2}{3}(y^{3} + 1)^{3/2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= [2(y^{3} + 1)^{3/2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= 2(2)^{3/2} - 2 \\\\\n&= 4\\sqrt{2} - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128944 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f52a0604-77f9-51c5-948a-710c52c26fe7" :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration on a Partitioned Region"}] :question/id #uuid "3477b5ca-309c-5b2a-b3fd-41dfc9bbf4db" :question/math-academy-id "e-7195" :question/problem "Change the order of integration in the double integral $\\displaystyle \\int_0^1\\int_{2x}^{3-x} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}y\\,\\textrm{d}x$." :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1997/Source/Images/d5f8a21d03c9e61e36a15cd45faecbfa.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. This is a type I region, and its type I representation is\n\n$$\nD=\\big\\{(x,y) \\::\\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\quad 2x \\leq y \\leq 3-x \\big\\}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we need to split $D$ into two type II regions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1997/Source/Images/4e6985e72db38bb32ef0c05926b8ba59.png)\n\nNotice that the right boundary of the lower region, written in the form $x=x(y)$, is\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{y}{2}\n$$\n\nAlso, the right boundary of the upper region, written in the form $x=x(y)$, is\n$x=3-y$.\n\nSo, we can write $D$ as a union of\n\n$$\nD_1 = \\Big\\{(x,y) \\::\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 2, \\quad 0 \\leq x \\leq \\dfrac{y}{2} \\Big\\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nD_2 = \\Big\\{(x,y) \\::\\: 2 \\leq y \\leq 3, \\quad 0 \\leq x \\leq 3-y \\Big\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{1}∫_{2x}^{3 - x}f(x, y)dydx &= ∫_{0}^{2}∫_{0}^{y/2}f(x, y)dxdy + ∫_{2}^{3}∫_{0}^{3 - y}f(x, y)dxdy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128945 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb8c80f2-6dcd-568e-812b-91d39a40119c" :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration on a Non-Partitioned Region"}] :question/id #uuid "eab89095-f5e5-53dc-9141-0f1a876f8a13" :question/math-academy-id "e-7196" :question/problem "Change the order of integration in the double integral\n$\\displaystyle \\int_0^1 \\int_{y^3}^{1} f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}x \\: \\textrm{d}y$." :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1997/Source/Images/25d522dde8ecc0a0a3957dae03108ac8.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. It is a type II region, and its type II representation is\n\n$$\nD= \\big\\{(x,y) \\::\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq1, \\quad y^3 \\leq x \\leq 1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nNotice that the top boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y)$, is\n\n$$\nx = y^3\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y = \\sqrt[3]{x}\n$$\n\nand the bottom boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny = 0\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as a type I region. The type I representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD= \\big\\{(x,y) \\::\\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\quad 0 \\leq y \\leq \\sqrt[3]{x} \\big\\}\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1997/Source/Images/925312e310acb44953022570595a9942.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{1}∫_{y^{3}}^{1}f(x, y)dxdy &= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{x}}f(x, y)dydx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129613 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f52a0604-77f9-51c5-948a-710c52c26fe7" :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration on a Partitioned Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e65d4d89-6d9f-5938-bd12-c05375041c1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{y}^{4 - y}∫_{0}^{4}f(x, y)dxdy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127473} {:answer/id #uuid "3bea747c-b1e6-500d-9358-f8d54bfa636b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{4}∫_{y}^{4 - y}f(x, y)dxdy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127474} {:answer/id #uuid "45d72daf-a2a8-53fe-8d22-e55ff2a889c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{4}∫_{4 - y}^{y}f(x, y)dxdy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127475} {:answer/id #uuid "76b11e55-10f0-5cd6-95d2-6ede8ffec86a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{2}∫_{y}^{4 - y}f(x, y)dxdy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127476} {:answer/id #uuid "7744d5a0-a627-5305-a4aa-8d3b166e4daf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{2}∫_{4 - y}^{y}f(x, y)dxdy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127477}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76b11e55-10f0-5cd6-95d2-6ede8ffec86a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{2}∫_{y}^{4 - y}f(x, y)dxdy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127476} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec08bfd6-54f4-5bc4-b805-d9be13c4db0d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127472}] :question/id #uuid "51957ec1-4df6-5139-b864-fabbb3776e09" :question/math-academy-id "q-38601" :question/problem "By changing the order of integration in the following double integrals, express the following sum as a single double integral.\n\n$∫_{0}^{2}∫_{0}^{x}f(x, y)dydx + ∫_{2}^{4}∫_{0}^{4 - x}f(x, y)dydx$"}]}
